  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA fairly active evening of convection is beginning to slow down \nacross eastern Kansas as the main shortwave axis having slid east \ninto northwest MO. Storms that developed off the high Plains over the\npast 6 hours have tracked into central KS and will continue to move \neast through the early morning hours. Given how worked-over the \nenvironment is, expectations are for these storms to be sub-severe, \nbut could see some gusty winds as they continue a weakening trend and\neastward progression. Scattered showers and weak thunderstorms will \nalso be expected to develop near the 850mb boundary that is \ncurrently near the I-35 corridor. Recent radar analysis and satellite\nobservations confirm this as convection has continued to develop \nover the past hour or two. Again, not expecting these to be severe, \nbut a storm or two could briefly become strong with gusty winds as \nthe main concern.\n\nThe remainder of the day will be much cooler in comparison to the \nprevious days with ample cloud cover and weak CAA limiting afternoon \nhighs to the upper 60s and low 70s. Weak mid-level ascent should \npersist across the region through the day today that will keep \nisolated to scattered chances for showers and a few thunderstorms in \nthe area. Highest precipitation chances should remain across far \neast-central KS, closest to the low-level boundaries. That said, \nsevere weather is not expected.\n\nMonday - Saturday:\n\nEnsemble guidance is in overall decent agreement in building a \nstrong surface high southward from the plains into the Midwest \nregions through mid week. Meanwhile, broad upper ridge axis spreads \neast into the region, returning dry and cooler conditions. Extensive \ncloud cover behind the front and persistent cold advection will \nmaintain the well-below-average highs down into the upper 60s and \nlower 70s through Wednesday. The upper ridge translates eastward by \nThursday as a northern stream perturbation enters the region. Low \nchance pops also return Thursday into Friday (20-50%) given the \nvariances on timing of the system and subsequent precipitation \ncoming off the western high plains.",
